<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syssitia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as syssition, pheiditia, andreia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The syssitia were the common meals of men and youths in social or religious groups in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, but also in Megara in the sixth century BCE and Corinth in the seventh. Tradition attributed their origin to Rhadamanthus, the legendary lawgiver of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>. In Sparta, where they were called pheiditia, the daily meal was obligatory even for the two kings; men were admitted from the age of twenty by a vote with balls of bread, a single flattened ball rejecting the candidate. Each member contributed a monthly share of barley, wine, cheese, figs and money, and those who could not pay were excluded. The main dish was the black soup.</p></div>
